{"max_tokens":4096,"messages":[{"role":"user","content":"{"claim":"Because screen time was measured before later developmental scores, the All Our Families analysis proves that screen exposure causes developmental impairment.","id":"q-bbc556c3e270d1c6","packet":{"evidence":"The All Our Families cohort analysis used data from 2,441 mothers and children in Calgary, Canada, at child ages 24, 36 and 60 months. Mothers reported screen time and ASQ-3 developmental screening performance. A random-intercepts cross-lagged panel model controlled stable between-person differences. Higher screen time at 24 months was associated with lower screening performance at 36 months (standardized beta -0.06, 95% CI -0.10 to -0.01); higher screen time at 36 months was associated with lower performance at 60 months (-0.08, -0.13 to -0.02). Exposure was observed. This uses corrected estimates: the 2019 correction clarified the model and corrected/swapped displayed coefficients and confidence intervals without changing the conclusions.","source":{"doi":"10.1001/jamapediatrics.2018.5056","title":"Association Between Screen Time and Children's Performance on a Developmental Screening Test.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30688984/","version":"Indexed primary abstract body sha256:8fe07f2717336b2673391ed02a2c8229ff790d0e3a64473cf82bd55cffb76be3; publication 2019-03-01; online corrected version, correction 10.1001/jamapediatrics.2019.0515"}}}"}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","system":"Classify the claim using only the supplied evidence packet and the common label definitions. Select one permitted label. Report confidence as a number from 0 to 1 representing the probability that your selected label is correct, and provide the concise explanation required by the common output schema. Return only the required JSON object.

Common task dictionary:
Choose one label for the supplied claim relative to the supplied evidence packet.
solid: Claims accurately report the evidence with appropriate scope and caveats. Weak, observational, preclinical or mixed evidence can be reported accurately.
overclaim: The conclusion exceeds the evidence. This is the residual category for inflated framing, unsupported positive or negative generalization, extrapolation to unstudied populations, and surrogate endpoints presented as clinical outcomes.
correlation_vs_causation: A causal claim is drawn from a non-causal, uncontrolled or confounded design; correlation is treated as causation without the needed counterfactual/comparison.
mechanism_vs_evidence: A described biological mechanism (an animal or preclinical pathway) is used to imply human clinical benefit not demonstrated by the evidence. This includes animal-to-human therapeutic and preclinical-pathway-to-therapeutic leaps. A biomarker/surrogate-to-clinical gap alone is excluded from this category.
Precedence: A specific label (correlation_vs_causation or mechanism_vs_evidence) takes precedence over generic overclaim only when its defining trigger is present and captures the core error. Do not stretch a specific definition; otherwise use overclaim. If the claim is appropriately bounded by the evidence, use solid.

Common output schema: exactly one JSON object with these fields:
final_answer: one of solid, overclaim, correlation_vs_causation, mechanism_vs_evidence;
evidence_tier: one of cell, animal, biomarker, limited_human, clinical_human;
conclusion_tier: one of same as evidence, human_therapeutic, human_policy, general_public;
bridge_supported: boolean;
assumptions, steps, alternative_hypotheses, uncertainties, key_claims, self_check: arrays of concise strings (empty arrays allowed);
confidence: a finite number in [0,1], the probability that the selected label is correct.
